  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nLook for lingering clouds and isolated snow showers tonight and\nFriday as an upper trough slides east of the region. This will\nlead to cold morning temperatures in the teens and low 20s by\nFriday morning. Meanwhile, NW winds are expected to increase again\non Friday, but focusing especially a bit east near the MT/ND\nborder closer to the departing trough. \n\nColder temperatures will linger into Saturday morning before\nensembles show rising 500mb heights over the region over the\nweekend and early next week with a building ridge pattern. The\noverall large scale subsidence will lead to dry weather conditions\nand an emerging warmer temperature regime. Highs by Tuesday \nacross NE Montana may push into the 70s or even 80s.\n\nEarly indications suggest that while this ridge axis slides\ndownstream by the middle of next week, diffluent southwest upper\nflow may set up over the Northern High Plains with the next\napproaching low pressure system. This could set the stage for a\nmore active weather pattern unfolding for the middle or second\nhalf of next week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNBM was largely followed for the forecast cycle given the low\nimpact snow showers tonight and overall quiet weather unfolding\nfor the weekend and into early next week. While it handled trends\nwell, it may prove conservative on the backside of the upper ridge\nfor Tuesday. The only real tweak was to nudge winds a bit higher\nfor Friday given the gusty NW winds expected, though the strongest\nwinds will be concentrated more to the east than on Thursday.\n\nConfidence is also growing that we may see a more active weather\npattern toward the middle of next week, though uncertainties\nremain at larger time scales and the timing of the departing ridge\nas well as finer details.",
